{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering attempt to remove penalties for small amounts of illegal substances will end on Sunday, replacing decriminalization with stricter enforcement. The new legislation, known as HB4002, transforms possession of drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth into a criminal offense once again. Individuals caught with these substances now face either prosecution or an alternative path: completing a behavioral health program and enrolling in a diversion initiative to avoid legal consequences. Under the revised law, possessing drugs for personal use will be classified as a misdemeanor, carrying a maximum sentence of six months in detention.\n\nThe updated measure is designed to empower law enforcement to address public drug use more aggressively, while also introducing tougher consequences for vendors operating near public spaces such as parks. Counties are encouraged, though not required, to establish treatment programs to redirect individuals away from the justice system and into addiction or mental health care. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions to their available resources, whereas critics fear it could lead to inconsistent and unfair enforcement across the state. Portland’s police chief notes the strict qualifications for diversion, such as having no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, or history of violent behavior, as well as being medically stable.\n\nIf someone qualifies for diversion, officers will relay their details to a county hotline to assess fit and arrange contact with a mobile outreach team specializing in behavioral health. These teams must respond within 30 minutes; if they fail to arrive on time, the person will be taken into custody. Chief Day emphasized that police cannot delay arrests while waiting for support services to arrive.\n\n***P.P.S.*** While debates over drug policies unfold, consider how societal approaches to substance use intersect with daily life—whether in the routines of a busy afternoon, the choices people make at a local market, or even the subtle shifts in conversation sparked by legal changes. Age-old traditions, like how a culture discusses or addresses personal struggles, are increasingly shaped by evolving laws. The evolving landscape may leave some questioning how far-reaching these adjustments truly are, much like wondering whether a shared meal or a quick shopping trip might feel different in the wake of such transformations. The very words exchanged, the tone of the tongue used to frame these discussions—all are part of the broader dialogue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.509902934543788e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.09533794224262238}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8368415832519531}, {"token": " attempt", "logprob": -3.123399496078491}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.07077766954898834}, {"token": " remove", "logprob": -1.1859674453735352}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.27873945236206055}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.0013063953956589103}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.1778521537780762}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.7310373187065125}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.731181277544238e-05}, {"token": " illegal", "logprob": -1.0861281156539917}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.09601566940546036}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.9165544509887695}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.2873743176460266}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -1.49888014793396}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.007292438298463821}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.36938419938087463}, {"token": " replacing", "logprob": -1.9160789251327515}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -1.5248335599899292}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.003393250983208418}, {"token": "ization", 